Screen Mirroring is not as Easy as it Seems: A Closer Look at Older Adults’ Cross-Device Experience Through Touch Gestures
Xiwen Ouyang a, Jia Zhou b, and Honglian Xiang b
aChina UnionPay Co. Ltd, Shanghai, P.R. China; bDepartment of Quantity Surveying, School of Management Science and Real Estate, Chongqing University, Chongqing, P.R. China
ABSTRACT
Screen mirroring might be a way to improve older adults’ user experience of smart televisions (STVs) through smartphones. To examine this possibility, two experiments were conducted. Experiment I examined older adults’ difficulties of screen mirroring (mirroring smartphone screens to STVs) through five common touch gestures (“Drag,” “Slide,” “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite”), in comparison to younger adults. The results indicated that a major problem for older adults is the frequent attention switching between the STV and smartphone screens. Therefore, experiment II explored how to reduce the need of attention switching through the touch gestures (“Tap,” “Slide + Tap,” and “Slide + Release”) and the button sizes (8, 14, and 20 mm) for different input postures. Thirty older adults participated in this experiment and their eye movements were tracked. Four major findings were derived. First, the “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite” gestures in screen mirroring were difficult for older adults with a task completion rate lower than 68%. Second, the problem of frequent attention switching between the STV and smartphone was predominant for tapping tasks. Third, the “Slide + Tap” and “Slide + Release” touch gestures helped to reduce attention switching in tapping tasks more than the “Tap” for older adults, while the “Slide + Release” received the worst subjective feedback. Fourth, increasing the button size from 8 mm to 14 mm on smartphones can improve the task completion rate and the task efficiency in screen mirroring when older adults used the one-handed posture to tap.

1. Introduction
Cross-device interaction might be an alternative to improve older adults’ interaction with STVs. Older adults recently can get more enjoyments by the advancement of STVs with the increased interactivity and rich resources. However, their user experiences with STVs are hindered by the traditional physical remote control because of its incomprehensible indirect inter­ action (McLaughlin et al., 2009), elusive button functions and complex labels (Rice & Alm, 2008), uncustomizable layout, and easily losing (Regina & Michael, 2014). Fortunately, these problems of the physical remote control can be avoided by using cross-device interactions instead. The cross-device interaction becomes an ubiquitous and convenient function due to the well equipment of Apple’s Airplay or Miracast in most new mobile devices. It can be used to display contents of smartphones onto STV to enjoy the large STV screen, clear viewing experience, and direct manipulation (Sun et al., 2015), and to share the screens with others (Mcgill et al., 2014).
Among various methods of cross-device interactions, screen minoring might be potential to bring a better STV experience to older adults. The cross-device interactions between smartphones and STVs consist of the screen mirror­ ing (mirrored UI) and the second screen (distributed UI) according to previous reviews (Brudy et al., 2019). As for the screen mirroring, users can use it to view the activities or media contents of smartphones on the STV screen with the

direct manipulation, the simple screen relations between smartphones and STVs (the display are the same), and the rich resources of smartphones. As for the second screen, the applications of it are usually some companion ones to enhance users’ tangential browsing or social viewing experience, or to act as a remote control (e.g., virtual remote applications or others, such as Apple TV Remote app) (Neate, Evans, et al., 2017). However, the second screen applications may rarely involve older people because they are too complicating for them, and the virtual remote still has the problem of indirect interaction, which cause some problems such as the inefficient focus manipulation (Ouyang & Zhou, 2018a), text input, and menu navigation (Bernhaupt et al., 2008; Carmichael, 1999; Ouyang & Zhou, 2018b). Thus, we focused on the screen mirroring in this study.
However, screen mirroring might cause new problems for older adults. The interaction with two screens simultaneously increases the difficulty of interaction and the attention dis­ traction (Rashid et al., 2012a; Sun et al., 2015), especially for older adults. In order to allow older adults fully enjoy the advantages of screen mirroring, it is essential to find the specific critical problem of screen mirroring and then to provide the solutions.
Touch gestures are the most important and commonly used input method on smartphones; however, few researches focus on the user experience of touch gestures in the screen

