mirroring. Considering the touch gestures make users able to directly manipulate the interfaces and help users to complete more complicated tasks intuitively (Sun et al., 2015), it is meaningful to investigate the performance and critical usabil­ ity problems of screen mirroring under the touchscreen ges­ tures for older adults. Thus, in this study, we focus on improving the usability of screen mirroring based on the touch gestures for older adults.
To achieve this goal, two experiments were conducted. Experiment I was conducted to investigate older adults’ diffi­ culties with touch gestures in screen mirroring. Based on the results, the critical problem identified was that older adults had to switch their attentions frequently between the smart­ phone and STV screen, especially when tapping on the touchscreen. Then, experiment II was conducted to verify possible solutions to this identified problem.
2. Literature review and hypotheses
2.1. The screen mirroring of cross-device interaction
Cross-device interactions become popular in recent years. According to a Google survey, 49% of users hold smartphones while watching TV (Google Inc., 2012). Similarly, 61% of Chinese users use TVs to watch popular online videos, which showed that users’ cross-device interaction behaviors are becoming more common (China Internet Network Information Center, 2015). The cross-device interaction between smartphones and STVs can be divided into two types by temporal interface distribution according to the pre­ vious reviews (Brudy et al., 2019), namely, screen mirroring and second screen. With screen mirroring, the contents between smartphones and STVs are the same, while with second screen the contents are different.
The screen mirroring is a common function, which is used to mirror the smartphone screen to an STV in real time via a wired connection or a wireless transmission protocol such as DLNA, Miracast, or Airplay (Mcgill et al., 2014; Sørensen et al., 2015), or sometimes to mirror the STV screen into a smartphone in real time (Kim et al., 2013; S. Kim et al., 2013). It has two usage scenarios. Firstly, it is usually used to share users’ smartphone screen with family, friends, etc., such as completing collaborative browsing tasks or consensus dis­ cussions in meetings with STVs (Mcgill et al., 2014, 2015). Secondly, users can use it to mirror the activities or media contents of the smartphone screen onto the STV for a clear view, large-screen immersive experience and directed manipu­ lation (Sun et al., 2015), such as presenting photos and videos.
The second screen usually uses some companion applica­ tions to enhance user’s viewing experience of STVs. These companion applications can be divided into three broad areas: supporting tangential browsing, supporting social viewing, and extending the remote control (Neate, Jones, et al., 2017). As for the extension of remote control, there are some virtual remote applications (e.g., Apple TV Remote app and Mi Remote app) in the consumer market, and other customizable remote which removes the interactive element from TV and transfers it to the second screen applications to control TV (Cruickshank et al., 2007). However, these extensions may increase the

difficulty of interaction and did not solve the problem caused by the indirect control of physical remote control, such as the troublesome focus manipulation (Ouyang & Zhou, 2018a) and menu navigation (Ouyang & Zhou, 2018b).
The screen mirroring has two advantages to further improve the user experience of STVs other than second screen, especially for older adults. Firstly, the interaction between the smartphone and STV of screen mirroring is easier and more intuitive for older adults because of the same contents and simple relationship between two screens than that of second screen. Secondly, with screen mirroring the contents that allows users to view are rich than that of second screen. Because screen mirroring is able to conve­ niently project all the contents of smartphones to STVs, while virtual remote control is usually used to manipulate the con­ tents on the STV. Currently, the applications and resources on smartphones (e.g., Tweet, Facebook, WeChat) are more abun­ dant than that on STVs thanks to the development of mobile internet. Therefore, it is possible that the screen mirroring might be able to bring better STV experience to older adults.
However, the screen mirroring also has usability problems. Firstly, when there is more than one screen, users have to spread their attention to different screens, and excessive atten­ tional switches can cause significant user performance degra­ dation (Rashid et al., 2012a; Sun et al., 2015). Secondly, older adults need to complete much excessive cognitive processing because the interaction with STVs is not direct control (Charness et al., 2004; McLaughlin et al., 2009). Thirdly, the increasing number of screens means a larger interaction scope which may result in more interference factors (Rashid et al., 2012a). So far, previous research has mostly focused on some applications and system construction of screen mirroring; however, few studies have considered the interactive pro­ blems, especially for older adults.
2.2. Touch gestures on the touchscreen
Touch gesture input is the most important and commonly used input method for smartphones, but there are few studies focusing on the touch gestures in screen mirroring. Because the interaction of screen mirroring just happens on the touchscreens of smartphones, touch gestures in screen mir­ roring have no difference from that on the touchscreens. Following that, the literature of this part was referred to the studies about touch gestures on smartphones only. Researches on touch gestures can be divided into two categories accord­ ing to the screen size: small (e.g., smartphones) and large touchscreens (e.g., ATMs, information kiosks).
As for the touch gestures on small touchscreens, Kobayashi et al. (2011) found that older adults could complete dragging, pinching without panning, and pinching with panning tasks successfully unless the tapping buttons were smaller than 30 pixels. A possible explanation is that the ability of older adults to deal with details, move accurately (Fisk et al., 2009), as well as their time controlling (Leonardi et al., 2010) may have declined, preventing them from meeting the motion require­ ments of tapping. Empirically, older adults do have difficulties with the tapping gesture. They tend to press a virtual button on the touchscreen as hard as pressing a physical button,

