leading to invalid taps (Chêne et al., 2016) or unintentional taps (Liu & Zhou, 2018). Additionally, once users need to tap a button in screen mirroring, they have to shift their atten­ tions from the STV to the smartphone, which causes an unpleasant delay (Cruickshank et al., 2007; Dezfuli et al., 2014). The attentional switches between STVs and smart­ phones would obviously become very troublesome for users with the increasing number of taps.
As for the touch gestures on large touchscreens, a study investigated the usability of clicking, dragging, zooming, and rotating and their design features for both older adults and younger adults (Gao & Sun, 2015). The results showed that although older adults’ performance of four touch gestures was worse than that for younger adults, older adults’ attitudes toward these touch gestures were more positive. Accordingly, these touch gestures might have a positive effect on the user experience of screen mirroring for older adults, but its effect still needs further investigation.
2.3. Solutions to relieve attentional switches of screen mirroring
As a result of Experiment I, the frequent attentional switches in the screen mirroring under the tapping task were found problematic for older adults. Although there are some studies offered some solutions to this problem, they focused on managing user’s attention of the second screen (Neate, Jones, et al., 2017), measuring users visual attention patterns (Vatavu & Mancas, 2014), surveying factors influencing visual attention switches without offering solutions (Rashid et al., 2012b). Thence, it is necessary to find the possible solutions to this problem, which could be divided into three ways.
The first way is to let users only focus on the STV screen and to use a smartphone as a second screen, such as a normal virtual remote control (Ouyang & Zhou, 2018a), the eye-free PalmRC (Ouyang & Zhou, 2018b), the system S3TV with a series of dedicated touch gestures (Sun et al., 2015), or the Touch&Screen with dedicated widgets that use simple touch gestures (Bellino, 2016). However, these solutions had some problems for older adults, for example, difficulty in remembering the gestures, complexity of operation, or inefficiency of indirect operations, which may not be suitable solutions for older adults.
The second way is to let users only focus on the smartphone screen, such as screen casting. In this way, users mainly interact with smartphones, and STVs are only used to present the final content. However, the contents of screen sharing in this method are limited, and the advantages of a large STV screen are not fully utilized. At the same time, older adults still face the challenges of using smartphones. For example, the small smartphone screen is hard to see clearly (Zhou, Rau, et al., 2014), and it is easy to unintentionally touch because users’ fingers block the screen or older adults’ finger flexibility is low (Chêne et al., 2016).
The third way is to allow users to focus on the STV and smartphone screen at the same time and provide solutions to reduce the attentional switches of screen mirroring. There are two possible solutions: increasing button sizes and improving tapping methods.
With regard to the button sizes, a lot of researchers focus on the effect of button sizes on touchscreens but few on the

effect of that on the screen mirroring. The researches of button sizes on touchscreens are summarized in Table 1. As for small touchscreens (i.e., smartphones and tablets; screen: 9.7 inches and below), the button size suitable for older adults should be at least 8 mm and is best in the range of 14–17.5 mm. As for large touchscreens (i.e., PDA, ATM, etc.; screen: above 9.7 inches), the suitable button size for older adults ranges from 15.9–20 mm; 20 mm is also recom­ mended by a study involving younger adults (Colle & Hiszem, 2004) and a study involving disabled people (Chourasia et al., 2013). For screen mirroring, there is no direct design guide­ line for button sizes that are appropriate for older adults to provide to practitioners. Considering the frequent attentional switches in screen mirroring, it could be speculated that the suitable button size for older adults might be larger than those on small touchscreens and that the button sizes on large touchscreens would be more appropriate. Therefore, we investigated the performance and satisfaction of the button sizes ranging from 8 to 20 mm; in addition, a middle size, 14 mm, was selected. The hypotheses are as follows:
H1 & H2: When older adults use screen mirroring (mirror­ ing smartphones screen to STVs), among the 8, 14, and 20 mm target button sizes on the smartphone screen, the 20 mm button size has the best tap performance (H1a) and satisfaction (H1b), while the 8 mm button size has the worst tap performance (H2a) and satisfaction (H2b).
With regard to the tapping methods, this study aimed to propose new touch gestures instead of the direct tap to improve the user experience of screen mirroring. The direct tap method is commonly used on touchscreens and needs users to look at the touchscreen and accurately determine the position of fingers. However, in the screen mirroring, every time users look down at the touchscreen, it causes a shift of attention, which may increase the risk of uninten­ tional touch for older adults. In this study, there might be two possible improved touch gestures more suitable for the screen mirroring. First, the “Slide + Tap” method divides the gesture into sliding to select a target and then tapping the screen anywhere to confirm this selection; this has been suggested by Chêne et al. (2016) who used the Drag & Swipe two steps on touchscreens to help older adults reduce unintentional touch. However, the “Slide + Tap” method might increase the task completion time because of the increase in interaction steps. Second, the “Slide + Release” method only requires users to slide to select a target and then release their fingers to confirm this selection; this is referred to the study of Jung and Jang (2015) who designed the “Press and Flick” to relieve the unintentional touch of the hyperlink for younger adults (20–26 years old). Compared to the “Slide + Tap,” the “Slide + Release” actually has only one step, which may lead to a shorter task completion time. The hypotheses are as follows:
H3: When older adults use screen mirroring (mirroring smartphones screen to STVs), the tap performance (H3a) and satisfaction (H3b) of the “Slide + Tap” are higher than that of the “Tap.”

