H4: When older adults use screen mirroring (mirroring smartphones screen to STVs), the tap performance (H4a) and satisfaction (H4b) of the “Slide + Release” are higher than that of the “Tap.”
Input postures may affect the tap performance. There are two common input postures to use smartphones: two-handed and one-handed postures. The two-handed posture is com­ monly used when sitting in a seat or lying in bed with two hands free (Chen, 2014). By contrast, the one-handed posture is used when the user has only one hand free, for example, when holding the handrail with one hand on the bus. Between the two postures, the former is mainly operated with the index finger, while the latter mainly uses the thumb. However, there are differences in finger sizes, degrees of freedom, and move­ ment abilities between the index finger and thumb (Zhang et al., 2005). For example, the interphalangeal joint of the thumb is wider than the distal joint of the index finger (Chinese Academy of Standardization and Information Classified Coding, 1996), but the degree of freedom of the thumb is greater than that of the index finger (Cash, 2011). Nevertheless, there is a lack of studies that consider the different input postures when discussing the effect of button sizes on the tap performance. A similar study indicated that younger adults who used the thumb had a better performance than who used the index finger, but there was no significant difference in satisfaction (Chen, 2014). The related hypothesis is as follows:
H5: When older adults use screen mirroring (mirroring smartphones screen to STVs), the tap performance with the two-handed posture is higher than with the onehanded posture.
3. Experiment I
An experiment was conducted to investigate the difficulties for older adults in using touch gestures (“Drag,” “Slide,” “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite”) in screen mirroring. For further investigation, the eye-tracking and age effects were considered in this experiment.
3.1. Variables
The independent variables were touch gestures (withinsubject) and user groups (between-subject). The user groups included older and younger adults. There were five touch gestures, including “Drag,” “Slide,” “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite,” selected in Experiment I. Following these pre­ vious studies (Cichoń et al., 2013; Gao & Sun, 2015), the tapping, dragging, sliding, pinching, swiping, zooming, rotate, etc., were investigated for older adults, which were proved not too difficult for them to use. The touch gestures should be selected according to some possible tasks in screen mirroring, such as changing a element’s location, browsing a web page, sharing a photo, drawing a mark to emphasize, and inputting some text with users’ smartphones. The first three tasks involve three commonly used touch gestures: “Drag,” “Slide,”

