and “Zoom” (Brudy et al., 2019). The last two involved more difficult touch gestures: “Draw,” and “Handwrite” (considering Chinese older adults are prone on using handwriting to input Chinese characters) (Chen, 2014), which were chosen to broaden the range of the available tasks for older adults and find critical problems as many as possible.
The dependent variables were performance and satisfac­ tion. Performance had three measures: task completion rate, task completion time, and attention ratio (on the STV screen). Task completion was defined as a participant completing a task without assistance. The task completion time was automatically recorded by the experimental system, starting from the time when the “Start” button was pressed on the task start page and ending at the time when the “Complete” button was pressed on the task end page. The attention ratio was used to measure the participants’ concentration on the STV screen or the smartphone screen and was collected by the Ergoneers eyeglass eye-tracking system, which was equal to the total gaze time on the STV screen divided by the total time of the task. Satisfaction included overall satisfaction, a ranking of the task difficulty, and overall satisfaction of using screen mirroring or using only a smartphone. Overall satisfaction was measured by an After-Scenario Questionnaire (ASQ) with three items using 7-point Likert scales (Lewis, 1995). The ranking of the task difficulty was sorted by participants after completing all tasks. Furthermore, to compare the experience of the touch gestures in using screen mirroring and using only a smartphone, the participants were asked to score the satisfaction of these two use conditions on a 7-point Likert scale. In addition, eight covariates were considered: age, gender, education, smartphone experience, smartphone usage time, dominate hand, dominate finger, and preferred posture with a smartphone.
3.2. Participants
A total of 14 older participants (five males and nine females; 63–78 years old) and 14 young participants (five males and nine females, 20–26 years old) were recruited for the experi­ ment. The older participants were from an urban area in Chongqing, and the younger participants were students from Chongqing University. All participants were able bodied without any limitations on their hand movement and had not taken any long-term intense exercise (e.g., playing basketball and badminton) before the experiment to avoid the impact of hand fatigue. Other details are shown in Table 2.
3.3. Prototypes and tasks
