The “Drag” task required users to drag the green rectangle to the green box for five trials. In each trial, the positions of the rectangle and box were randomly changed. Participants needed to confirm their finger’s position at least once in each trial; this occurred when the drag started.
The “Slide” task required users to slide through the web page to find the specified number and to choose it. There were three different web page sizes (3600, 7200, and 14400 pixels) to increase the difficulty in sliding. There were six trials in total. At least one confirmation of the finger’s position was required in each trial; this occurred when the participants selected the target at the end of the task.
The “Zoom” required users to zoom the small red box to coincide with the large green box for two trials. Participants had to confirm their fingers’ position at least twice in each trial; this occurred when the participants placed two fingers on the screen and moved the picture with a single finger. However, the difficulty in completely overlapping two boxes led to more than one confirmation.
The “Draw” required users to draw a circle, a tick, and a cross in the white tracks for two trials. The fingers’ position needed to be confirmed at least three times in each trial because the participants need to determine the appropriate starting points. As the number of adjustments increased, the number of confirmations increased.
The “Handwrite” required users to trigger the input box and then handwrite the appointed Chinese characters for two trials. The appointed characters in the tasks were chosen from the previous study to reduce the influence of the characters (Chen, 2014). The fingers’ position needed to be confirmed at least twice in each trial; this occurred when triggering the box and starting to write. The number of confirmations increased as the number of written strokes increased.

