3.4. Apparatus
The experimental equipment is shown in Figure 2. To ensure the screen mirroring between smartphone and STV to run smoothly, the screen of a smartphone (iPhone7; screen: 4.7 inches; resolution: 1334 × 750; OS: iOS 12; role: input device) was mirrored onto a laptop computer (Xiaomi; reso­ lution: 1920 × 1080; OS: Windows; role: like a set-up box) by the Airplay through the AirSever Connect application. And a 60-inch STV (Sharp; screen: 60 inches; resolution: 1920 × 1080; role: display device) was used to display the computer screen via an external HDMI cable. The experiment was conducted in a quiet office, and the participants sat on a sofa 3 m away from the STV.
An Ergoneers eye-tracking system developed by Ergoneers GmbH of Germany was used to collect the attention ratio on the STV and smartphone screen. This system included Dikablis Professional Glasses and D-Lab (version 3.0). There are two eye cameras and one field camera set up on the glasses. The tracking frequency of the eye camera is 60 Hz per eye and the pupil tracking accuracy is 0.05° visual angle. As for the field camera, the resolution is 1920 × 1080 pixels (Full HD) and the aperture angle is from 40° to 90°. With the D-Lab, the eye-tracking data were collected and analyzed in the experiment. The markers in the field videos can be con­ nected with Area of Interest (AOI) of the STV and smart­ phone screen dynamically to obtain the attention ratios on the

STV and smartphone screen. However, due to the small scope of smartphone screens and the limited aperture angle of the field camera, it was difficult to capture the users’ attention on the smartphone screen accurately. Therefore, the attention ratio of the STV screen was used to measure users’ concen­ tration on the STV screen rather than that of the smartphone screen.
3.5. Procedure
The experiment took about 50 minutes. First, the participants were given the informed consent and background information questionnaires. Second, the experimenter helped participants to put on the eye tracker and then calibrated it. After this, the screen brightness of the smartphone was turned down and participants were told to mainly look at the STV screen to prevent them from only staring at the smartphone. Third, the participants were trained and then asked to complete five touch gesture tasks independently and fill out the ASQ scales after each task. To avoid the effects of dominate hand, finger, and posture, participants were asked to use two hands to hold the smartphone (left hand to hold and right hand to operate). Last, the participants were required to rank the difficulty of the five touch gestures and then participate in a brief inter­ view. The interview required participants to evaluate their

