4.1. Variables

4.3. Apparatus

The independent variables were the tap methods, button sizes, and input postures. The tap methods had three levels: “Tap,” “Slide + Tap,” and “Slide + Release.” The button sizes included three levels: 8 mm, 14 mm, and 20 mm. Both variables were within-subject. There were two levels of input postures (between-subject): two-handed and one-handed postures.
The dependent variables were performance and satisfaction. The performance was measured by three dimensions, includ­ ing task completion rate, task completion time, and attention ratio (STV screen), which were the same as experiment I. Satisfaction included perceived ease of use, attention to use, and overall satisfaction. The perceived ease of use and attention to use were measured by the Post-Experiment System Availability Questionnaire (PSSUQ) with 7-point Likert scales (Lewis, 1995; Zhou, Zhang, et al., 2014). Additionally, the overall satisfaction was subjectively rated by participants via a one-question questionnaire with a 7-point Likert scale (Sauro & Dumas, 2009; Tedesco & Tullis, 2006). As for the covariates, besides the eight covariates included in experiment I, finger dexterity was added because it might affect the time of tapping for older adults.
4.2. Participants
A total of 30 older participants (eight males and 22 females) were recruited for the experiment. The participants were from an urban area in Chongqing, China. The participants were in good health, except for one participant who reported having Parkinson’s syndrome. Although this participant had a tremor in his hands, he had no difficulty in performing the tasks independently. Other detailed information of the participants is presented in Table 6.

The experimental system with 3 (button sizes) × 3 (tap meth­ ods) types of interfaces was developed with Android 5.1 as shown in Figures 8 and 9. Each interface contained six but­ tons in the middle of the screen with a gap of 5 mm between elements, and the buttons were numbered from 1 to 6 ran­ domly without repetition. As for the tap methods, the “Tap” method is no different from that on smartphone touchscreen. While the improved “Slide + Tap” method has two steps to complete a tap (slide the finger to focus a target then release and tap anywhere to confirm), and the “Slide + Release” method has only one step (slide the finger to focus a target then release to confirm). In both improved methods, a fingerprint was used to indicate the current location of the user’s finger.
The equipment used in experiment I was also involved in experiment II (see Figure 10), including the eye tracker. In addition, a finger dexterity tester was used to measure parti­ cipants’ finger dexterity by recording the total time spent by the participants to place iron needles into small pinholes. The way to mirror the smartphone screen to the STV was similar as experiment I, except that the smartphone was changed to an Android phone (Huawei; screen: 5.2 inches; resolution: 1080 × 1920; OS: Android 6.0) and the mirroring application was changed to the ApowerMirror application (Room 345, third Floor, Peninsula Center, 67 Mody Road, Tsim Sha Tsui, Kowloon, Hong Kong) in order to meet technical require­ ments as well as ensure the experimental system run smoothly. Given that data of the two experiments were not compared to draw conclusions, the influence of devices and applications might not be significant.
