5. General discussion
5.1. Major findings
This study sought to investigate the critical problem for older adults in interacting with screen mirroring between an STV and a smartphone and offer solutions to this problem. The results showed that the difficult touch gestures would hinder the user experience of the screen mirroring for older adults. Specifically, except for the “Drag” gesture, the performance and satisfaction of using “Slide,” “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite” in screen mirroring were worse than using only on a smartphone screen. One critical problem making these touch gestures difficult in screen mirroring was that users had to frequently switch their attention between the STV and smartphone, which was also mentioned in the previous stu­ dies (Bellino, 2016; Dezfuli et al., 2014; Rashid et al., 2012a; Sun et al., 2015). Considering that the “Tap” gesture was the foundation of other touch gestures and led to the obvious

