attentional switches due to the need for accurate positioning, the top priority to improve the user experience of screen mirroring is to relieve the problem of frequent attentional switches when tapping.
Using improved tap methods was an effective way to relieve the attentional switches of screen mirroring. Older adults using the improved touch gestures (“Slide + Tap” and “Slide + Release”) had significantly higher attention ratios (68.0% and 66.8%) than using the normal “Tap” (49.0%). Furthermore, the improved tap method did not significantly harm the user performance even when the operation steps were increased in terms of task success and time consumption. Affected by users’ habits, the “Tap” method obtained the highest satisfaction, and the “Slide + Release” method obtained relatively high satisfac­ tion scores. However, increasing the size of the buttons from 8 mm to 20 mm did not play a part in reducing the attentional switches in screen mirroring for older adults.
It should be noted that this result of the “Slide + Tap” was quite different from the previous studies. In this experiment, the “Slide + Tap” method received the worst feedback, whereas the previous studies suggested that it was useful for older adults to divide the “Tap” into two steps (i.e., selecting and confirm­ ing, Chêne et al., 2016; Jung & Jang, 2015; Liu & Zhou, 2018). There may be two reasons for this difference. First, the use scenarios were different. Liu and Zhou (2018) research focused on learning scenario instead of daily use, which emphasized the learning effect other than performance. Second, the purposes of use were different. Jung and Jang (2015) purpose was to alle­ viate the problem of accidental touch when users tapped the hyperlinks in web navigation (Jung & Jang, 2015). Conversely, the targets in our experiment were buttons, and they were not easily touched by mistakes. Therefore, when tapping the but­ tons on smartphones in the daily use of screen mirroring, the “Slide + Tap” method is not recommended for use when designing a screen-mirroring interface for older adults.
The input posture had an effect on the tap performance in screen mirroring. Using the one-handed posture had a lower task completion rate and longer task completion time than using the two-handed posture, which may result from the differences of the fingers for two aspects. First, the thumb mainly used by the one-handed posture is generally shorter and wider than the index finger mainly used by the two-

handed posture (Chinese Academy of Standardization and Information Classified Coding, 1996); thus, the motion cap­ ability of the thumb is lower than index finger and the response time is also longer (Chen, 2014). Second, when using the onehanded posture, the user’s range of motion is limited to one corner of the device screen (bottom left or bottom right) (Karlson et al., 2006). While the two-handed posture involves the fingers, wrists, and arms, making the range of motion more flexible than that of the one-handed posture.
Increasing the button size improved the performance and satisfaction when older adults used the one-handed posture to tap in screen mirroring. The smallest button size (8 mm) with the one-handed posture had the worst performance and satis­ faction. In particular, the task completion rate using the 8 mm button size was 8.6% lower and task completion time was 40.7% higher than when using 14 mm with a one-handed posture. In a similar study, the researchers pointed out that when older adults used smartphones to complete the tap tasks, the task completion time when tapping the 12 mm buttons was 13.0% lower than when using 8 mm buttons (Hwangbo et al., 2013), which may mean that tapping in screen mirroring was more difficult than only on a smartphone. However, using the 20 mm button was not significantly different from using the 14 mm button in terms of performance, which meant that there is no need to use too large buttons in screen mirroring.
5.2. Practical implications
For practitioners, it is better to take care of the attentional switches between an STV and a smartphone in screen mirror­ ing for older adults. Frequent attentional switches increase attention consumption and interaction difficulty of the touch gestures in screen mirroring. Specifically speaking, it is recommended that screen-mirroring interfaces are designed to avoid these difficult touch gestures as much as possible, such as the “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite” tasks. More importantly, it is essential to provide solutions to reduce the difficulty of touch gestures in screen mirroring to allow older adults to fully enjoy the advantages of STVs with a large screen. Starting from the most basic aspect, improving the basic tap gesture is a promising solution.

INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF HUMAN–COMPUTER INTERACTION

1187

The “Slide + Release” tap method is recommended to be added into the design of screen-mirroring applications instead of only providing the “Tap” method. The “Slide + Tap” and “Slide + Release” were both able to alleviate the problem of attentional switches in screen mirroring for older adults. Although the subjective feedback for these touch gestures was worse than that of the “Tap” method because of its familiarity and intuition, the “Slide + Release” method was acceptable when weighing both the performance and satisfac­ tion. Considering that the “Slide + Release” method was learned for the first time in this experiment, it might be able to become preferred after continuous use. Thus, to maximize the user experience of the screen-mirroring application, there is a need to provide both the “Tap” and “Slide + Release” methods for users and provide operation instructions for users who are using “Slide + Release” for the first time.
In screen mirroring, using one hand to hold the devices and tap buttons caused performance reduction for older adults, and there might be two ways to solve this problem. The first way is to change from one hand to two hands. However, the use of postures is determined by the users’ personal habits and usage scenarios. In some cases, users have only one hand free, for example, when holding a pen, operating a mouse, turning over books in a meeting, or hold­ ing chopsticks or a soup spoon at home. Previous surveys suggest that most smartphone or PDA users prefer to use one hand to input because then they are able to handle other tasks at the same time (Karlson et al., 2006). Therefore, requiring users to input with two hands may not always be a good solution to improve the tap performance in screen mirroring. The second way is to adjust the interface design. Increasing the button size is a promising way to improve the perfor­ mance and satisfaction when users use the one-handed pos­ ture. It is best to avoid using small buttons (smaller than 8 mm) when designing screen-mirroring applications for older adults; buttons larger than 14 mm are recommended.
5.3. Limitations and future studies
Four limitations of this study are noted. First, certain gestures used in this experiment are abstract and challenging for older adults, which might apply to a boarder population as well. This may lead to limitations in generalizing these design recommendations to realistic tasks. Second, due to technical limitations, this study did not use the same smartphones and operating system for the test of prototypes. Third, the experi­ ments in this study only measured the initial use of the touch gestures and button sizes by older adults. It remains to be explored whether continuous use has different effects on these solutions when users tap the buttons in screen mirroring. Fourth, considering the importance and scope of the problem, this study only investigated tapping tasks. However, it is possible that the solutions in this study might have a negative impact on other types of tasks. For example, “Slide + Release” may conflict with the existing sliding ges­ tures. Future work may be expanded to explore the effect of the the “Slide + Release” method on other touch gestures and to provide improved schemes for other common touch ges­ tures in screen mirroring.

6. Conclusion
This study aimed to investigate the difficulties about touch gestures of screen mirroring for older adults through Experiment I and to find solutions to the major problems (i.e., the frequent attentional switches between the STV and smartphone screens when tapping) via Experiment II. This study then attempted to solve this problem by improving the touch gestures (“Slide + Tap” and “Slide + Release”) and increasing the button sizes (8, 14, and 20 mm).
There were four findings. First, using the “Zoom,” “Draw,” and “Handwrite” gestures in screen mirroring was difficult for older adults; these had task completion rates lower than 68%. Second, the major problem of the screen mirroring for older adults was the frequent attentional switches between the STV and smartphone screens, which became obvious when tapping on the screen. Third, the improved “Slide + Tap” and “Slide + Release” touch gestures led to higher attention concentration (on STV screen) of screen mirroring by 30.3% and 28.3% than the “Tap” for older adults when completing tap tasks. Considering the “Slide + Tap” obtained the worst satisfaction, it is recommended to provide both the “Tap” and “Slide + Release” methods for older adults to choose in screenmirroring applications. Fourth, the tap performance with the one-handed posture in screen mirroring for older adults exhibited 3.8% lower task completion rate and 27.9% longer time than that with the two-handed posture, which can be improved by increasing the button sizes. In particular, the 14 mm button size on the smartphone had a 8.6% higher task completion rate and 40.7% lower time than the 8 mm button size when older adults used the screen mirroring with one-handed posture.
